WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.800
For about 60 years, insulin for people with diabetes came mostly from the pancreases

2
00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:11.320
of cattle and pigs.

3
00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:17.140
In 1982, the FDA approved human insulin made by bacteria.

4
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.640
Bacteria have no human insulin gene.

5
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.460
So how does a human gene get into a microbe?

6
00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:29.160
And can we design life, and how should we decide when we should?

7
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.720
Genetic engineering means using laboratory tools to change an organism's DNA.

8
00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:42.760
DNA is the same kind of molecule in every living thing, so a cell can often read a

9
00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:44.020
gene from another species.

10
00:00:45.080 --> 00:00:50.620
A restriction enzyme is a protein from bacteria that cuts DNA at one particular

11
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:56.880
short sequence. Many restriction enzymes leave short single-stranded tails, called

12
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.880
sticky ends.

13
00:00:58.683 --> 00:01:04.403
A plasmid is a small, usually circular piece of DNA in a bacterium.

14
00:01:04.843 --> 00:01:07.743
It copies itself apart from the main chromosome.

15
00:01:08.663 --> 00:01:13.383
Cut it open with the same enzyme that cut the gene, and the sticky ends match.

16
00:01:14.403 --> 00:01:19.383
DNA ligase is an enzyme that joins two DNA ends into one strand.

17
00:01:20.083 --> 00:01:24.943
The result is recombinant DNA: DNA from more than one source.

18
00:01:25.967 --> 00:01:28.267
Put the plasmids into bacteria.

19
00:01:29.027 --> 00:01:31.087
Only some bacteria take one up.

20
00:01:31.827 --> 00:01:35.847
The plasmid usually carries a gene for resistance to an antibiotic.

21
00:01:36.627 --> 00:01:41.627
So growing the bacteria with that antibiotic leaves mostly the ones that took it.

22
00:01:42.267 --> 00:01:46.027
As they divide, the plasmid is copied, gene and all.

23
00:01:46.827 --> 00:01:50.927
If the gene is switched on, they make the insulin chains, which are collected,

24
00:01:51.327 --> 00:01:52.667
purified and joined.

25
00:01:53.517 --> 00:02:00.157
The polymerase chain reaction, or PCR, makes many copies of one chosen stretch of

26
00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:04.897
DNA. In an ideal reaction, the amount doubles each cycle.

27
00:02:06.197 --> 00:02:09.417
Gel electrophoresis sorts DNA pieces by size.

28
00:02:10.297 --> 00:02:11.597
Shorter pieces travel farther.

29
00:02:12.857 --> 00:02:18.897
CRISPR uses a guide RNA that matches one sequence, and a protein called Cas9 cuts

30
00:02:18.897 --> 00:02:19.937
both strands there.

31
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.830
So can we design life?

32
00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.770
With these tools, people can change DNA.

33
00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:33.230
Bacteria make human insulin because they copy a plasmid and read the human gene on

34
00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:39.490
it. Related tools now change crops, and CRISPR now edits blood stem cells.

35
00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:46.310
Whether a change is worth making is judged use by use, by evidence and by values

36
00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:49.350
such as safety, fair access and limits.
